WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.930
Sales, stock and credit: know what you sold, and who owes you.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.420
This is the video for Module 2, Sales, stock and credit records.

3
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:15.800
Your sales book is the most used page in your business. If you only keep one record, keep this one.

4
00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:21.550
Your stock card shows where most of your business money is sitting: on the shelves.

5
00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:31.310
And your debtors list gets your money back. Credit keeps customers loyal, but it is the fastest way for a small business to run out of money.

6
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:39.880
Each sale gets one line: date, item, quantity, price, total, and how it was paid. Cash, transfer, or credit.

7
00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:43.080
For example, Funke sells foodstuff in Ibadan.

8
00:00:44.330 --> 00:01:01.210
She sells 4 paint buckets of garri at about 3,500 naira for cash, that is 14,000 naira. She also tallies 12 small cups of crayfish at about 500 naira, another 6,000 naira. Her cash sales are 20,000 naira.

9
00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:08.670
Two buckets of beans at about 5,000 naira are paid by transfer. That is 10,000 naira.

10
00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:15.510
And one bottle of palm oil goes to Iya Sade on credit, 2,500 naira.

11
00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:30.090
All sales come to about 32,500 naira. Now she knows exactly what to check: 20,000 more cash in her purse, 10,000 more in her account, and Iya Sade in her debtors list.

12
00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:35.140
At closing, step one. Add the Total column for the whole day.

13
00:01:36.390 --> 00:01:43.640
Step two. Count your cash and take away the float, the change money you started with. The rest should equal your cash sales.

14
00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:54.510
Step three. Open your bank or wallet app and tick each incoming transfer against a line in your book. Unmatched alerts often mean a forgotten sale.

15
00:01:54.860 --> 00:02:01.710
Always confirm the money is in your own account before goods leave. Never trust only the customer's phone screen.

16
00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:14.710
Step four, check every credit sale is in your debtors list. And step five, write any difference with a short note. Short now and then is normal. Short almost every day means something is wrong.

17
00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:23.300
Opening stock, plus stock bought, minus stock sold, equals what should be left. Count what is really left and compare.

18
00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:30.140
For example, Esther sells soft drinks by the crate in Port Harcourt. On Monday she counts 10 crates.

19
00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.550
She buys 15 more on Wednesday, so 25 crates were available.

20
00:02:36.800 --> 00:02:41.090
On Saturday she counts 7 crates. So 18 crates left the shop.

21
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:49.460
But her sales book shows only 17 sold. One crate, about 6,000 naira at cost, is unexplained.

22
00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:59.520
Her helper explains: 2 bottles broke and some were given to visitors. Now Esther writes breakages and gifts in the Out column, and the gap disappears.

23
00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:11.840
Count at the same time each week, for example Saturday after closing. Write breakages, spoilage and goods taken for home in the Out column. Sell older stock first.

24
00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:21.320
And never sell food, drinks or medicines past their expiry date. Remove them, record them as spoiled, and buy smaller quantities next time.

25
00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:29.570
Write every debt in front of the customer and read it back with the due date. Record each part payment and the new balance.

26
00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:39.840
For example, Chioma sells fabric in Aba. At month end, Ada, Joy and Uche owe her 73,000 naira. Uche's debt is 7 weeks old.

27
00:03:41.090 --> 00:03:52.590
Next week's stock will cost about 120,000 naira, and she has only 60,000 in hand. So she calls Ada today, agrees a date with Joy, and stops credit to Uche.

28
00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:59.230
Sort debts by age and chase the oldest first. The longer a debt waits, the harder it is to collect.

29
00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:12.700
Set your rules. Credit only for regular customers you know, a limit per person, a clear payment date, no new credit until the last debt is paid, and no credit sale without a line in the list.

30
00:04:13.950 --> 00:04:23.980
Now run a full sales book day, build your weekly summary, start a stock card for your top product, and clean up your debtors list. Continue with the lessons below.
